\title{poolinfo: Reliability-Profile Diagnostics for Partial Pooling}
\description{
\if{html}{\figure{logo.svg}{options: style='float: right' alt='logo' width='120'}}

Summarizes how much a set of estimates with known standard errors, such as the site estimates of a multisite trial, the studies of a meta-analysis or the direct estimates of small areas, can say about the individual units under a normal hierarchical model. Computes the site reliabilities and their informativeness, mean reliability, dispersion and concentration, with posterior means and intervals from numerical integration over the between-site standard deviation, design calculations, plots made with 'ggplot2', and tables and sentences for reporting. The diagnostics follow Lee (2026). Supported by the Institute of Education Sciences through Grant R305D240078 to the University of Alabama.
}
\details{
The diagnostics describe a set of site reliabilities. A site's
reliability is the proportion of the variance of its estimate that comes
from the variation of the true effects; under the normal hierarchical
model it is the weight of the site's own estimate, against the mean of
the effects, in the site's posterior mean.

The main functions:
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=reliability_posterior]{reliability_posterior()}} computes the posterior of the diagnostics
from estimates and standard errors, by numerical integration over the
between-site standard deviation.
\item \code{\link[=reliability_profile]{reliability_profile()}} computes them at a given between-site standard
deviation, or at the estimate of a metafor fit.
\item \code{\link[=reliability_draws]{reliability_draws()}} computes them from posterior draws of another
fit, such as one of brms.
\item \code{\link[=reliability_design]{reliability_design()}}, \code{\link[=balanced_allocation]{balanced_allocation()}} and \code{\link[=solve_design]{solve_design()}}
plan a trial.
\item \code{\link[=compare_profiles]{compare_profiles()}}, \code{\link[=report_table]{report_table()}} and \code{\link[=report_text]{report_text()}} compare and
report analyses.
\item \code{plot()} and the functions \verb{plot_*()} show them.
}

\code{vignette("poolinfo")} works through an analysis, and the other
vignettes cover the profiles, the posterior summaries, a multisite trial,
meta-analysis, small area estimation, planning and the computation.
}
